(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["AdjacencyHypermatrix", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"adf6e054-95ad-a744-9e68-ca6e04e554d5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"AdjacencyHypermatrix", "[", "hg", "]"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"525e58b8-4a37-e04d-b61c-93dc034dd74d"],
 " ",
 "gives the adjacency hypermatrix of the hypergraph hg: one array for each \
arity and symmetry type of edge it has, held in a Hypermatrix."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"8a01fc96-d9cb-1d4c-b0b1-d0b44e28ceee"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"e515b063-15ed-174b-ad45-f277824cc9fc"],

Cell["\<\
An edge of arity k contributes to an array of order k, whose indices run over \
the vertices in the order VertexList[hg] gives them. The entry at an index \
tuple is the number of edges of that group sitting on it, so a repeated edge \
counts twice, the hypergraph being a multiset.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"346a357b-2b0b-6849-8921-92c5dfb3500b"],

Cell["\<\
The symmetry types of the two halves of the package correspond one for one, \
and this is where that correspondence is written down: a \"Directed\" edge \
gives a \"Rigid\" array, a \"Cyclic\" edge a \"Cyclic\" array, and an \
\"Unordered\" edge a \"Symmetric\" one.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"86d25bb6-3e38-0949-9cf7-92444716a431"],

Cell["\<\
An array carries one symmetry and one order, so the edges are gathered by \
arity and symmetry type together and each group becomes one array. A \
hypergraph with edges of the same arity but different symmetry types \
therefore gives several arrays of the same dimensions, one per type.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7e16ed5a-474c-af46-9b04-3dbda2035e7e"],

Cell["\<\
The entry is looked up against the canonical index of its tuple, the least \
one in its orbit under the symmetry. Every tuple of an orbit therefore reads \
the same count, which is what makes the array carry its declared symmetry by \
construction rather than by luck.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"736abcfa-7093-1d4e-aef1-5c3c34428ec6"],

Cell["\<\
Identical copies of an edge share one entry, so the count there is an integer \
multiple. That holds at every symmetry type: two copies of a cyclic edge give \
2 at each of its three rotations, three copies of an unordered one give 3 at \
each of its six permutations.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b3e2a69c-50c8-9048-b24c-5f3e9394406d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "By default the entries count edges and labels play no part, so a labelled \
hypergraph gives the same hypermatrix as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["HypergraphSkeleton", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"60016971-b6a6-cf4c-ad79-aecdc1e75b79"],
 " of it. With ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Labeled\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"895f524c-5fb9-2742-a6d8-de4ff174629d"],
 " the entries are the edge labels themselves, and 0 where there is no edge; \
an edge carrying no label of its own gives ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["None", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "03022eb5-8f66-084f-b260-6d3408a9badf"],
 ", which absence does not."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"3808d567-6047-1642-b707-43aa5b58ab05"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Labels as entries need the hypergraph to be simple, since one entry cannot \
hold two labels, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"AdjacencyHypermatrix", "::", "nonsimple"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"eeb8f2fe-2070-3b45-88fe-792c4e6cc72f"],
 " is given if it is not. Counting has no such trouble: two edges in one \
place is a 2. See ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["SimpleHypergraphQ", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"ed417d03-5a0a-704b-bd7b-b0840d73faaf"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"fdda6397-1be2-5440-ad01-29ff796eeb31"],

Cell["\<\
A label fills the whole orbit of its edge, just as a count does, so the array \
carries its declared symmetry either way. The value domain of a labelled \
array is \"Label\", which is never inferred and has to be declared, so that \
an array of labels is always one on purpose.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"4cd562cf-1d8d-6a47-8f20-3baa733d3115"],

Cell["\<\
A hypergraph with no edges gives an empty hypermatrix. An edge of arity zero \
gives an array of order zero, whose single entry is the number of such edges.\
\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1e0cf42a-99d4-974f-b8ed-e7b52fc2ec56"],

Cell["\<\
The vertices of a hypergraph are a set and have no order of their own, so the \
numbering of the indices is a choice. It is made by the Hypergraph \
constructor, which stores its vertices sorted, and not by \
AdjacencyHypermatrix: giving the same edges in a different order therefore \
changes nothing.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"032cd8bd-aabf-ad45-a4c8-7b414c66a479"],

Cell["\<\
What that buys is determinism, not invariance. The sort is on the vertex \
identifiers, so renaming the vertices can permute the indices, and two \
isomorphic hypergraphs may well give different hypermatrices \[LongDash] \
differing by the simultaneous permutation of all their indices that the \
renaming induces. As an invariant of the isomorphism class an adjacency array \
is defined only up to such a permutation, which is the usual state of affairs \
rather than a defect.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"ce541e74-3452-2e43-b114-7378623e2eb1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For an invariant, compose with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["CanonicalHypergraph", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"05f334e8-d622-6049-a948-e7ded249daff"],
 ", which relabels the vertices one to n by isomorphism class: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"AdjacencyHypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"CanonicalHypergraph", "[", "hg", "]"}], "]"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"7c95fe3e-f60a-e846-a7b2-b5587b406a20"],
 " depends on the class alone. It is left as a composition rather than given \
a name of its own, so that it is always clear which of the two is meant."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9a82ea8d-b1d2-f349-9bed-16e19ea8fd20"],

Cell["\<\
Nothing is lost: the canonical entries of the arrays name exactly the edges \
that went in, with their multiplicities, so the hypermatrix determines the \
edge multiset of the hypergraph.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"96c10097-d450-934a-bcc4-7600d3267a36"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"0fdfa174-57fc-8441-89ef-75190a54b74c"],

Cell["\<\
A hypergraph with edges of two arities and two symmetry types:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"f38619ed-6014-5747-932a-3726384f5de6"],

Cell["\<\
h = Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2, 3}, \"Unordered\"], Edge[{3, 4}, \"Directed\"],
                Edge[{1, 2}, \"Directed\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2f9d766a-c958-6f4a-8a05-b1955eacfdd4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 GraphicsBox[
  NamespaceBox["Hypergraphs",
   DynamicModuleBox[{Typeset`hg = HoldComplete[
     WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Hypergraph[{
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[4]}, {
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2]}, "Directed"], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[4]}, "Directed"], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3]}, "Unordered"]}]]}, 
    {AbsoluteThickness[Medium], Opacity[0.5], Arrowheads[{{Medium, 0.5}}], {
      {Opacity[1], Arrowheads[{{Small, 0.5}}], 
       {Hue[0.63, 0.7, 0.5], Opacity[
        0.7], {ArrowBox[
          BSplineCurveBox[{{1.9987657369935588`, 1.061628456550387}, {
           1.9987444371974448`, 0.}}]], {}}, 
        {Opacity[1]}}}, 
      {Opacity[1], Arrowheads[{{Small, 0.5}}], 
       {Hue[0.63, 0.7, 0.5], Opacity[
        0.7], {ArrowBox[
          LineBox[{{1.256851436554468, 0.5308254495580791}, {0., 
           0.530777583334723}}]], {}}, 
        {Opacity[1]}}}, 
      {Hue[0.63, 0.66, 0.81], Opacity[0.1], 
       FilledCurveBox[{
        BSplineCurve[{{1.9987444371974448`, 0.}, {1.8751044786719961`, 
          0.5308160984890078}, {1.9987657369935588`, 1.061628456550387}}], 
         BSplineCurve[{{1.9987657369935588`, 1.061628456550387}, {
          1.689631228511252, 0.6635224608785275}, {1.256851436554468, 
          0.5308254495580791}}], 
         BSplineCurve[{{1.256851436554468, 0.5308254495580791}, {
          1.6896259035622236`, 0.3981153467409308}, {1.9987444371974448`, 
          0.}}]}], 
       {Opacity[1]}}}, 
     {Opacity[1], 
      {Hue[0.63, 0.26, 0.89], EdgeForm[{Hue[0.63, 0.7, 0.33], Opacity[0.95]}],
